---
import PageNav from "./PageNav.astro";
import TeachConcept from "./TeachConcept.astro";
import TeachFacts from "./TeachFacts.astro";
import { PIE_PALETTE, smallestPieSpec } from "../data/teach/pie";
import { playgroundHref, specText } from "../data/teach/spec";

const href = "/docs/charts/pie";
const spec = specText(smallestPieSpec);
const play = playgroundHref(smallestPieSpec);
const gap = `{
  "mark": { "padAngle": 1.5 }
}`;
const labels = `{
  "mark": { "labels": true, "labelPosition": "outside" }
}`;
const legend = `{
  "legend": { "position": "bottom" }
}`;
const palette = `{
  "mark": {
    "palette": ${JSON.stringify([...PIE_PALETTE])}
  }
}`;
const sort = `{
  "mark": { "sort": "desc" }
}`;
const start = `{
  "mark": { "startAngle": 90 }
}`;
const tooltipOff = `{
  "tooltip": false
}`;
const tooltipOn = `{
  "tooltip": true
}`;
---

<p>
  A pie shows how a few categories add up to one total. Each category is a slice of a solid disk. There is no hole, and no total in the middle.
</p>

<section id="when-to-use">
  <h2>When to use it</h2>
  <p>
    Use a pie for parts of a whole, with a handful of categories. Two to six slices stay readable. A line is the chart for a time series. A bar is the chart for comparing totals, and for a long list of categories. Many thin slices turn the disk into a legend.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="donut">
  <h2>Pie and donut</h2>
  <p>
    Pie and donut share one arc renderer. The <code>type</code> selects the chart and its defaults.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>type: "pie"</code> is the solid disk. <code>mark.innerRadius</code> defaults to <code>0</code>, and <code>mark.padAngle</code> defaults to <code>0</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>type: "donut"</code> keeps the hole. <code>mark.innerRadius</code> defaults to <code>0.6</code>, and <code>mark.padAngle</code> defaults to <code>2</code>. The hole is where the total is printed.
  </p>
  <p>A hole is a donut. Set <code>type: "donut"</code>. This page does not offer a hole control.</p>
</section>

<section id="data">
  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    Each row is one category and one number. Point <code>encoding.x</code> at the category and <code>encoding.y</code> at the number. The channel is <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>. It is not <code>theta</code> or <code>angle</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers are amounts. ChartKit adds the slices it draws and gives each slice its share of that sum. You do not pass percentages. Share of revenue uses 42, 28, 18, 8, and 4. Plan mix uses 52, 24, 14, and 10. Loans by shelf uses 840, 310, 460, 520, 190, and 260.
  </p>
  <p>
    A missing number, a zero, or a negative number is left out of the disk. <code>validate()</code> still accepts those rows. If every row is left out, <code>render()</code> returns an error chart, CK1006, instead of a pie.
  </p>
  <p><code>a11y.alt</code> is required. One sentence, 10 to 500 characters, with the point of the chart.</p>
</section>

<section id="spec">
  <h2>A spec that renders</h2>
  <p>
    This is the smallest pie. <code>version</code>, <code>type</code>, and <code>a11y.alt</code> are required. The disk is solid and the slices meet, so <code>mark</code> can be omitted.
  </p>
  <TeachFacts type="pie" label="Pie packages" spec={spec} />
</section>

<p>Each option below is a key on the spec. The demo changes that key and updates the chart and the snippet together.</p>

<TeachConcept id="gap" title="Gap">
  <p>
    <code>mark.padAngle</code> is the gap between slices, in degrees, from 0 to 10. A pie defaults to 0, so the slices meet. The renderer spends that many degrees on the gap and shares the rest of the circle by value.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.padAngle</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{gap}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="labels" title="Slice labels and the legend">
  <p>
    <code>mark.labels</code> draws a value on each slice. It defaults to false. <code>mark.labelPosition</code> sets the place. <code>inside</code> and <code>center</code> sit on the slice. <code>outside</code> draws a leader line. Setting <code>labelPosition</code> turns the labels on. With no <code>mark.labelFormat</code>, the label is the share of the total. Overlapping labels are skipped.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.labels</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{labels}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <code>legend</code> is the key beside the disk. Omit it, or set <code>true</code>, and a pie with more than one slice shows a legend. <code>false</code> hides it. <code>legend.position</code> is <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>, or <code>inside</code>. When position is omitted, a chart at least twice as wide as it is tall puts the legend on the right. Otherwise the legend sits along the bottom. <code>legend.values</code> prints each slice's number next to its name. Kiln hours sets that.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>legend.position</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{legend}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="colors" title="Colors">
  <p>
    Omit <code>mark.palette</code> and each slice takes the next theme color. Set <code>mark.palette</code> to a list of colors, in slice order, to override that list. One to 24 colors. Loans by shelf sets six. <code>encoding.color</code> can name a field on the row. It is the same channel other charts use.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.palette</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{palette}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="sort" title="Sort">
  <p>
    <code>mark.sort</code> orders the slices. <code>none</code> keeps the data order and is the default. <code>asc</code> and <code>desc</code> sort by the number.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.sort</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{sort}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="start" title="Start angle">
  <p>
    <code>mark.startAngle</code> is where the first slice starts, in degrees. <code>0</code> is the top, and the slices run clockwise. The value can be from -360 to 360.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.startAngle</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{start}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</TeachConcept>

<TeachConcept id="tooltip" title="Tooltip">
  <p>
    <code>tooltip</code> is on for a pie when the key is omitted. Hovering a slice shows its name and its value. <code>tooltip: false</code> turns the card off. <code>tooltip: true</code> turns it on.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>tooltip false</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{tooltipOff}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>tooltip true</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{tooltipOn}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <code>tooltip.fields</code> chooses the rows in the card, and <code>tooltip.position</code> places it. Loans by shelf sets both. Change those in the playground. Turning the card off and on again puts that object back.
  </p>
</TeachConcept>

<section id="more">
  <h2>The rest of the spec</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.cornerRadius</code> rounds the slice corners, in px, from 0 to 40. <code>mark.other</code> folds slices under a fraction of the total into one bucket. <code>mark.explode</code> names slices to pull out, and <code>mark.offset</code> is how far, in px. <code>mark.innerRadius</code> opens a hole. That is a donut, so it is not a control on this page. Theme, number format, and the other shared keys live on the playground, with the full option list.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="related">
  <h2>Related charts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/donut">Donut</a> is the same slices with a hole. The spec type is <code>donut</code>.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/rose">Rose</a> gives every category the same angle and uses the radius for the value.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/radial-bar">Radial bar</a> puts categories around a circle and uses the length of the bar for the value.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={play}>Open this spec in the playground</a>. That link opens the spec on this page. The demo link opens the chart you are looking at, including any option you change.
  </p>
</section>

<PageNav href={href} />
